# Every frozen character this repository wants, one per line.
#
#   <name>  <key script>  <seed>  <settings file>
#
# The four fields are exactly the four arguments tools/make_char_fixture.sh
# takes, so a row IS the command that builds its fixture. Fields are separated
# by whitespace; a '#' starts a comment; blank lines are ignored.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. Before it, a fixture existed only if somebody had run
# make_char_fixture.sh by hand and remembered to, so the check suite could not
# be converted off seed-generated characters without a day of typing (bd
# inc-sls0). tools/sync_char_fixtures.sh reads this file, builds every fixture
# that is MISSING, and leaves every fixture that is PRESENT alone.
#
# THE JOB DOES NOT CHASE THE MODULE, and that is the point. A fixture exists to
# escape the rID-position defect: an rID is a POSITION, so one resource added
# to lib/ shifts every id above it and silently rewrites a seed-pinned
# character (bef32c3 moved the seed-1 Lizardfolk monk from STR 18 with a long
# sword +3 to STR 14 with a quarterstaff, on byte-identical dice). Regenerating
# a fixture after a module change therefore produces A DIFFERENT CHARACTER and
# retires every check built on the old one. Only a save-format change is a
# reason to regenerate, and the .sav declares its own format in its header.
#
# THE NAME ENCODES WHAT MAKES THE CHARACTER DIFFERENT: who he is, the seed, and
# the settings. Three settings files appear here and they are not
# interchangeable -- a fixture is only meaningful with the one that made it:
#   opt0822  tools/fixtures/options-2026-08-22.dat
#   opt0818  tools/fixtures/options-2026-08-18.dat
#   gate     tools/gates/Options.Dat
#
# To add a character: write a key script that ends by saving (see
# tools/keys/chargen-default-save.keys), add a row here, and run
# tools/sync_char_fixtures.sh. Never edit an existing row -- a fixture is
# frozen, and a check reads its expectations out of the fixture's own sheet.

